  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "M.Umesh vs Food Inspector on 21 March, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Monday, The 21St Day Of March 2016/1St Chaithra, 1938 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 1068 of 2006 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 97/1999 of Addl.Sessions Court (Adhoc)-Iii, Thalassery Cc 158/1996 of J.M.F.C.-Ii, Kannur Revision Petitioners/Appellants/Accused Nos.1 To 3: ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- 1. M.Umesh, S/O.Muthukumar Chettiar, Thilakam Nivas, Kattampally Road, P.O.Chirakkal. 2. Kanakaraj, S/O.Suppayya Chettiar, Arunkumar Ice, Manna P.O., Chakkarakkal. 3. V.Easwary, D/O.Marimuthu Chettiar, Thilakam Nivas Kattampally Road, Puthiyatheru Chirakkal. By Advs.Sri.N.A.Muraleedharan Sri.K.C.Peter Respondent/Complainant And State: -------------------------------------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector, Kannur Circle. 2. State Of Kerala, Represented By The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala Ernakulam. R1 & R2 By Public Prosecutor, Shri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On M.Umesh vs Food Inspector on 21 March, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/104777098/ 1 21-03-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: B.Sudheendra Kumar, J. - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - -- - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - Crl.R.P. No.1068 of 2006 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - Dated this the 21st day of March 2016 O R D E R",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 4.12.1995, PW1, the Food Inspector, purchased 24 pieces of ice fruit from the shop of the first accused. The accused Nos.1 and 2 were running the shop. The third accused was the licensee of the said shop. After sampling as per rules, one of the samples was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. The Public Analyst filed Ext. P11 report stating that the sample contained non-permitted coal tar dye Rhodamine-B and artificial sweetener saccharine and therefore, the same was adulterated. Thereafter, on the request of the accused, second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory for analysis. Ext.P14 certificate of the Central Food Laboratory would show that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for the food item and therefore, it was adulterated. It was also stated in Ext.P14 certificate that the sample contained non-permitted coal tar dye Rhodamine-B and artificial sweetener saccharine. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW4 were examined and Exts.P1 to P22 were marked for the prosecution. Ext.D1 was marked for the revision petitioners.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P14 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) M.Umesh vs Food Inspector on 21 March, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/104777098/ 2 (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P14 certificate of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioners and the learned Public Prosecutor.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P14 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) M.Umesh vs Food Inspector on 21 March, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/104777098/ 2 (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P14 certificate of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra).",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioners and the learned Public Prosecutor."
  },
